  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nLatest surface analysis places a stationary front from the northern \nGreat Lakes ESE through Long Island, NY with high pressure over SE \nCanada into northern New England. High pressure will keep much of \nthe area mostly dry tonight as a wave of low pressure starts to form \nalong the stationary front. Valley fog will be likely for portions\nof the CT Valley as well as northern valleys where radiational \ncooling will be more efficient.\n\nClouds will advance northeastward across NH late tonight as the\nwave of low pressure nudges the stationary front northward. Rain\nwill become likely across southern NH by day break with rain\nspreading northward Tuesday morning. Models are in reasonable\nagreement that high pressure will hold firm over SE Canada and\nwill limit rain chances north of a line from the White Mountains\nto KLEW to KRKD. Rainfall will generally be light, while some\nelevated instability combined with PWATs greater than 1.5 inches\ncould lead to some localized heavier rates over NH. Chances for\nrain will diminish Tuesday evening into Tuesday night from NE \nto SW as the wave of low pressure tracks SE offshore of Cape\nCod. Total rainfall will approach 0.75 inches across SW NH and\nwill diminish to the northeast. Otherwise it will be cooler\nTuesday with highs in the low 70s while northern areas that see\nmore sun could push into the mid 70s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA more widespread and robust round of showers and storms \napproach the area from the west on Wednesday. Most of the day on\nWednesday itself looks mainly dry, with the best chance for \nsome scattered showers across the higher terrain. By late in the\nday, the more widespread round of showers begins to push into \nwestern New Hampshire.\n\nA weak low pressure center gradually organizes as it moves \nthrough New England Wednesday night and Thursday. This likely \nbrings a round of steady rain and heavier showers to all of the \nforecast area, with the potential for a widespread 0.75 to 1.5 \ninches of rain, the most a lot of towns have seen in a while. \nThe steadiest rain looks to fall during the overnight hours \nWednesday and the first half of the day on Thursday. The chance \nfor showers persists through the afternoon on Thursday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\n\nOvernight Thursday night and Friday, a trough deepens across \nNova Scotia while the surface low pressure deepens as well. This\nconsolidates most of the unsettled conditions to the east of \nNew England by Friday, and leaves us on the western edge of the \ntrough. This results in a drier and cooler pattern with \nnortherly flow. The bulk of the heat under a large ridge \naffecting much of the eastern 2/3 of the country remains west of\nNew England. So while we won't break into the warmth, being on \nthe western edge of the trough brings a drier outlook for the \nweekend than previous model runs were suggesting from the last \nfew days.\n\nHighs in the 70s look to prevail for most of the area from \nFriday through at least Sunday, with nighttime lows in the 40s \nto low 50s. Being under the influence of the trough, mornings \nwill be the sunniest part of the day, with pop up clouds and \nscattered showers likely a feature each afternoon from Friday \nthrough Sunday. The chances for showers progressively lower each\nday, and are most likely across the higher terrain.",
